※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=1 diag=none

※ A hole written twice in one pattern matches only where the two are written
※ alike, which is how a rule says its arguments agree.  Two pieces of a program
※ are alike where the same thing is written in both, wherever each was written --
※ so the spans are not compared and `3u8` matches `3u8` from anywhere.

macro same:
    ⌜$x, $x⌝ → ⌜1u8⌝
    ⌜$x, $y⌝ → ⌜0u8⌝

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    ⎕narrow(same⌜2u8 + 3u8, 2u8 + 3u8⌝ × same⌜2u8, 3u8⌝ + same⌜4u8, 4u8⌝,
            ⌜u6⌝) ?? 1u6
